WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Whether it's singles or doubles, you'll love the non-stop action of this amazingly real tennis game.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.820
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.740
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.300
I'm Joe. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.080
And I'm Sam. Hey, all four of us here again.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.500
Lovely. Anyway, let's talk about tennis, boys.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.160
What do you think? Tennis boys.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.380
Oh, yeah. Well, we can talk about tennis boys.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.240
Let's stick with the game.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.580
I was ready to do a whole thing about Roger Federer.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.520
But anyway, yeah, let's talk about tennis.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.620
The NES game, a launch game, and kind of like, you know, a proving ground for any video game system.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.840
At this point, I'm not saying any more.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.240
You won't see the PS5 launch with a tennis game.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.340
Unfortunately, tennis games are kind of rare nowadays.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.860
You don't really see like a virtue of tennis or anything like that.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.340
Yeah. But back then, of course, tennis was a proving grounds thing.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.440
Pong obviously being a big one.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.540
But there were iterations of tennis before that.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.780
But Pong obviously like...

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:17.980
Oscilloscope. I'm sorry.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.040
Sorry. Oscilloscope tennis was the first video game.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.220
Yeah. And that's like...

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.320
And that's totally different than like what people think of video games.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.080
Like, honestly, you know, a lot of people probably didn't know that.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.640
So I would highly recommend looking that up.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.800
I know a lot of people know of like computer space and stuff like that in terms of older

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.760
video games than Pong.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:35.980
But this is really...

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:36.840
This is the 50s?

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.620
Yeah. This is really early.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.060
And I'll link it in the show notes.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.380
Literally unplayable. Yeah.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.660
But anyway, enough about that.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:48.920
But so Pong obviously ate like enough quarters to justify like so many other people to try

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.660
arcade games. And that's how you see stuff like Pac-Man.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.820
You know, I'm just going to list off Frogger or stuff like, you know, stuff.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.280
Obviously, it grew from there and then created a crash.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.980
And so that's not dangerous.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.820
And I'm not saying tennis games cause crashes because really games like E.T.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.220
cause crashes. Not that E.T.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.740
is really the worst game of all time.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.740
Have to get that off my chest.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.540
There's a lot of throwing things out there and taking them back all the way.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:13.800
Yeah, you know, I noticed that.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.700
But isn't that really just what tennis is?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.420
Ah, good point. You know, hitting a ball, getting it back to you.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.580
It's a concept episode.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.700
We're trying something new here, folks.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.040
But maybe we should just get into our first impressions.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.480
Joe? Yeah, well, first thing I noticed, the exact same music as baseball on the opening title

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.420
screen. Ooh.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.120
And yeah, that is true.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.600
And that can easily be explained by the fact that both Intelligent Systems and Nintendo

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.560
R&D 1 worked on both those games.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.600
Okay. Yeah.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.340
And other than that, I mean, I felt like it was tennis.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:01.260
I guess my first impressions are always just random observations, but the players scuttle.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.080
They're very bug-like when they scuttle across the court.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.520
So that creeped me out a little bit.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.660
But this is why we do first impressions.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.680
Yeah, it was tennis. So that was my initial reaction.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.960
Sean? I was not expecting you to be freaked out, like creeped out by tennis.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.380
But like, did you notice that they, like, even after the play, I don't know, I don't

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.340
play tennis, but after the play, like, your opponent scuttles up to the net.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.280
Oh, yeah. That is true. That is my first impression.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.940
I felt like the computer was, like, taunting me, because it usually did that after I made

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.180
a mistake. That's true.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.300
You just go, what? Yeah.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.340
Walk up to the net and just be like, what?

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.680
He didn't walk. He scuttled.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:48.860
I'm sorry. But yeah, I mean, other than, like, sort of anthropomorphizing the computer player,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.740
no, this is, again, this is tennis.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.140
Like, I dig old sports games.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:59.960
I thought this was more frustrating, even though it feels a little bit more intuitive

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.640
than a lot of these other games that we've played.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.680
I thought it was frustrating because it feels rigged.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.920
But that may just be me, because I'm not very good at games.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.000
But that's my first impression of tennis.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:18.160
Yeah. My first impression, it's kind of in the same as what you guys said, I feel like.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.820
I mean, actually, not as what Joe said.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.040
That was not my first impression.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.340
I didn't get scuttled.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.940
I thought you were actually alarmed if you were making the same analogy.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.920
I'm more just like, OK, yeah, this is a basic video game representation of tennis.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.600
And like, I can play it.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.840
And maybe I'll get better at this because I seem kind of bad at it right now.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:35.820
And maybe I won't.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:36.760
And we'll see what happens.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.160
Yeah, I think we all kind of.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.900
Oh, sorry. I forgot that you had to go.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.220
Yeah, Mike exists. Yeah.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.140
Our host. Thank you so much for welcoming me back to the show.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.280
You're welcome anytime.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:52.400
Thank you. So for me, I like tennis a lot as a sport and I like tennis games.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:05:03.520
And my initial impression was kind of that this was so different than what I imagine like game tennis games this time would be capable of, especially when we look at like what baseball is capable of and stuff like that.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.000
So this is actually a pretty competent game, in my opinion.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.560
But to the difficulty level.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:09.980
Yeah, the difficulty is there.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:18.620
And I actually like felt at every time like, oh, this is really like there's something on the line here because I could be on a 40 love streak.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.240
And then all of a sudden it's like, you know, we're a deuce.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.940
And then next thing I know, I've lost six sets.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:25.840
Yeah. So.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:31.460
So, yeah, first impressions for me were definitely like to Sam's point, how do we get better at this?

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.380
And my short answer is you really don't.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:40.280
Yeah. Let's just talk.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.380
This is sound silly, but I'm just going to go through the objective real fast for those of home that don't know.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:51.720
Tennis basically is, you know, one person serves the ball and then they hit it back and forth in a volley.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:06:02.100
And then if it goes out of bounds or if the other person fails to return it, the person gets 15 points and 15, 30 and then 40.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:03.520
So I'm sorry.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.620
I don't know the thing behind that.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:07.000
And then love means zero.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.540
Don't know the thing behind that either.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.560
Sorry. I'm really prove myself as a tennis person here.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:11.720
But you do that.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:19.380
And then after you get past the 40, you take the set and then you have to take six sets to get the game.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.720
And then you have to take two out of the three games.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:30.120
If that sounds long, you'd be surprised to know that in real men's tennis, you need to take three games to win.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.900
So it can go up to as many as six or seven games if necessary.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:43.140
You know, if we did this for like baseball and football, like if I pulled out the rule book for football.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.420
Yeah, it's interesting that the game kind of boils down to that.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:51.500
And obviously there's other things like faults and, you know, terminology that we could talk about.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.480
But I think that tennis is actually a pretty simple game.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.680
And that's kind of what makes it so accessible to video games.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.460
The idea of hitting a ball back and forth.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.080
Yep. At least in theory.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.200
So let's talk about singles mode first.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.540
And that's just kind of the standard tennis game that most people are familiar with.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.720
It's very much like Pong only now there's a couple of different enhancements, like different types of shots.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:25.100
Yeah. I don't know if I ever could pull off any other shot than just like overhand.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:26.300
Are you saying on purpose though?

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.700
Or like, did you ever notice?

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.060
Yeah. Cause I mean, it happens.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.000
They did. Like if I was closer to the net, it would go for like a, I don't know what the word is.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.580
Just like a slam or.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:37.540
Yeah. An overhead smash.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.020
I think the manual says smash.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.840
Yeah. Did you look at the manual?

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:41.840
I did look at the manual.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.260
Were you as confused as I was that it says like press a to return the ball.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.620
And then here are 12 different shots.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.460
How you do it.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.380
Like exactly. I guess it's just, it's a situation dependent when you do it.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.340
Right. And as far as I know, there's really only like four shots.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:01.200
There's like your normal hit, your lob, your smash, and probably like a backhand.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:11.860
Yeah. You know, the one thing that I, the main reason why I said that I thought that this game was rigged against me was because I don't know about how you guys feel about this.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.360
Maybe you had a different experience, but I felt like I had no control over where this ball went.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.520
Like it, it just always went back to the guy.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.040
I feel like it was in the timing of it.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.380
Like if you're early, it'll go a little bit off to the side and you're late, it'll go to the other side.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:31.160
But even, even that is like, I'm not, I'm not sure if that's exactly right.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.560
That's how I kind of felt.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:46.520
Yeah. The intuitive way to, to, to, uh, give the player input on where to hit the ball would be if you press right, the right button when you're returning.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:56.340
Yeah. And one thing that we're kind of dropping out here is that this game has a Z axis, if you will, in the sense that like, it really tries to show the air.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.260
But a lot of times that makes you just misjudge a lot of things.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.140
Yeah. But it's crazy.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:11.520
Yeah. It's crazy how much it boils down to like where you hit that ball on the Z axis, because to your point of like saying that you didn't feel like you had much control over the ball.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:22.600
I actually felt like even the smallest, the smallest difference in my timing of hitting that ball was going to register in like hitting the net or going out or.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:26.720
Yeah. But I'm saying like, so to me, that was what was crazier.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.360
It wasn't like so much like, I don't know where this ball is going to go.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.640
It's like, you have to be really precise.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.260
Not that there's anything wrong with that because in real tennis, you have to be.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:46.700
Yeah. Precision is key, but it's, it's a little frustrating in an early video game where you can't exactly tell how close that ball is to you on the Z axis, at least to hit the ball.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:52.220
Think that you're timing it perfectly and then watch it like do the smallest bounce in front of you.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.940
I also, I also felt like it had to do with where on your racket you hit it.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:02.100
Like if you, if you take a couple, couple extra steps away from the ball, you hit, it hits the edge of the racket.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.880
And then if you take a couple, you know, one step closer, it hits the middle of the racket.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:12.100
Uh, but I didn't really have any way of like testing that theory to see you like, because it's, it's how it's happening so fast.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:13.860
And I'm not particularly good at this game.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:23.100
So I feel like, uh, I feel like the biggest handicap for me anyway, it was just that your guy moves so fast that you can't ever get into the spot where you need to be that.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:29.240
I mean, I know that that's essential because once you get to the more harder difficulty levels, you have to move fast to get to where the ball is.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:33.860
Right. Right. Um, but even I went easy first and I went medium and then I went the hardest.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:42.180
I just, I feel like it was so I had to, I couldn't hold down a direction because I would just go way off, uh, like off to the left or something.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.640
So you felt slippery? Oh yeah.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.620
Not like slippery cause your guy stops on a dime.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.740
But you have to like kind of tap it really quickly to make a move instead of, instead of just hold down a direction.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:52.780
Cause I just could not get him where I wanted it to be.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.360
Yeah. To, uh, to Sam's point.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:09.160
Um, one of the reasons why I thought that soccer was such a good version of that sport in an eight bit game was because, uh, when you moved your player, it felt like there was traction.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.320
It felt like there was some sort of resistance.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:14.580
There was like, there was like kinetic energy to it.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:21.380
Uh, you, the only real time that you could control in which direction you were going was when you were with contact, made contact with the ball.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:32.700
So you would think that it wouldn't be that hard to sort of simulate the fact that you can't just run at full speed in one direction, trying to chase this ball.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:36.600
And then immediately go at the exact same speed in the other direction.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:44.280
You'd think that there would be some momentum that feeling of like, Oh, I have to slow down and then keep going.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.320
Like you have weight. Yeah. There's no weight to this.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.700
Yeah. So I mean like, I'll just add here cause this is, I feel like this is important.

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.860
You know, we were praising Shigeru Miyamoto in last week's episode, uh, for super Mario brothers.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.960
Like we were all over him, but he designed this game too.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.760
And he also designed baseball another game that we weren't too hot on.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:05.000
So it's kind of interesting to see at the early level of his career.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:06.900
I mean, we don't know. I mean, they list him as designer.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.300
We don't know how involved he was.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:11.140
I'm not saying he programmed anything.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:15.080
He didn't program anything, but you know, they put him in as designer for these games.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:21.120
And it's very interesting to see like, you know, these small little nuances that he doesn't quite have perfect yet.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:22.120
You know, he's not on this.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:23.920
He's not on this incredible run yet.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.120
You know, he's, he's not at super Mario brothers three level yet.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.900
I think he's just doing too many sports games recently.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:36.240
Yeah. And I think like, you know, again, these are games that are, you know, he wanted to, he designed a lot of the NES games.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:45.240
Like, uh, I think, you know, I'll put an article up on the, on the website eventually of like the Shigeru Miyamoto launch games and talk about them a little more in depth in relation to him.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:51.840
But overall, like he had input on almost every NES game that was coming out at that point because it was important.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:57.380
You know, it's, he was important in the Japanese world because each month a new Famicom game was coming out.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:05.700
So it was kind of like important of like, you know, it's not like the NES launch here where it's like, throw 17 games at them and this will keep them, you know, full for a little bit, you know?

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:12.140
So, uh, so I agree with everything that you're talking about here in terms of like player control and stuff like that.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:21.660
I just can't get over how even on like, and sure on difficulty one, it's not so tough, but like, it's always feels like a duel with the computer here.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.360
And that says something about the AI.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:31.380
Maybe it's a little like too fine tuned, but it's also like a little nice to see like a tennis game that just doesn't hand it over to you.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:42.600
Sure. I just felt like, you know, it's, it's important when at any point, if it's like 1530 or you're up 40 love, like at any point, the whole game can change.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:49.240
And while I think that's frustrating and I was screaming at the TV, I don't think that like, there's anything wrong with that.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.260
You know, it's, it's supposed to be challenging.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.820
I mean, tennis is not a game where you're just supposed to like win set after set after set.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.980
How rewarding would it be to win six, nothing, six, nothing game over?

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:11.020
It feels like, it feels like the kind of thing that if you did have this game for, you know, a month to just deal with this game and without being distracted by other games, you can get a lot better at it.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:11.940
Oh, for sure. Yeah.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:18.480
And kind of progress. So we should, we should talk about the difficulty levels a little bit.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.940
Oh, for sure. Joe, you want to kind of like walk us through some difficulty?

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:26.780
So, so, I mean, I'll be honest, I played the first of the easiest and the hardest mode.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:27.860
Didn't really play anything in between.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:31.560
And there is certainly a drastic difference, as you would expect.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:39.640
That's kind of crazy. There's a polar, you know, there's the hardest mode has features a sunburned guy who he is.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:41.280
I could not even imagine beating him.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.620
Yeah, I was going to say he seems more like a demon.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:46.640
They really programmed him.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:48.600
But yeah, he is.

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:50.300
I don't even think I scored a point on him.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:55.620
Whereas with the easy difficulty, I felt like it was pretty even, actually.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:00.460
So I guess I can see, granted, I didn't play the, you know, two through four difficulties.

